Nomi wrote:
>
> -Merge dagger, knife, short sword and saber skills into Short Blades

Dagger is now extremely useful.

Knife is only useful for a non-artifact game.  In addition to regular
knife there are worm tooth, crysknife (the reason the school is useful
in a non-artifact game), scalpel and stiletto.

Saber is a long blade that does not belong in this grouping.

> -Merge broadsword, long sword and scimitar into Long Blades

Currently scimitar is useless in most games.

> -Merge spear, trident, and polearms into Polearms, make them useful in
> melee without the need to ride

Add lance not spear.  But if you've ever seen any of these weapons in
use at SCA events you'll see why they are not supposed to be useful in
melee.  In demonstration combat it happens like in the game.  The one
with the halberd backs up circling trying to get out of close range to
use the halberd.  The one with the sowrd move forward trying to get into
sword range.  It really helps to have a blocker with a shield.

> -Merge axes and battleaxes into Axes

They already are.  Maybe you mean the two types of axe with the two
types of pickaxe.  An axe has an edge parallel to the shaft, an adze has
an edge perpendicular to the shaft.  Small hackets and adzes are camping
and carpentry tools.

> -Increase the rarity of long swords a lot, not being dropped by any
> kind of soldier

There is occasional discussion of moving several of the long sword
artifacts to other weapon types.  Currently there are two sabers, two
sorta-katanas, two hammers, three daggers, three broadswords, but only
one mace, axe, staff, bow, morning star.

Merging non-edged weapons would put the mace, hammers and morning star
together.

Maybe having Giantslayer and Dragonbane being spears would work.  Plenty
of similar shufflings have been proposed in the past.

> -Make a new skill, equivalent to "two weapon combat", "two-handed
> weapons" that improves the accuracy with a twohander
> -Make twohanders have somewhat painful (-7 or so) to-hit penalties

Those are already in place much as you describe.


